PropertyChanged.Fody causes unexpected behavior

The current documentation states that PropertyChanged.Fody performs the transforms:

```C#
public class Person : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;
    
    public string GivenNames { get; set; }
    public string FamilyName { get; set; }
    public string FullName => $"{GivenNames} {FamilyName}";
}
```

Into:
```C#
public class Person : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;

    string givenNames;
    public string GivenNames
    {
        get => givenNames;
        set
        {
            if (value != givenNames)
            {
                givenNames = value;
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.GivenNames);
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.FullName);
            }
        }
    }

    string familyName;
    public string FamilyName
    {
        get => familyName;
        set 
        {
            if (value != familyName)
            {
                familyName = value;
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.FamilyName);
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.FullName);
            }
        }
    }

    public string FullName => $"{GivenNames} {FamilyName}";

    protected void OnPropertyChanged(PropertyChangedEventArgs eventArgs)
    {
        PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, eventArgs);
    }
}

internal static class InternalEventArgsCache
{
    internal static PropertyChangedEventArgs FamilyName = new PropertyChangedEventArgs("FamilyName");
    internal static PropertyChangedEventArgs FullName = new PropertyChangedEventArgs("FullName");
    internal static PropertyChangedEventArgs GivenNames = new PropertyChangedEventArgs("GivenNames");
}
```

This is in fact misleading as the auto-generated setter for properties does not compare the new value against the backing field, but instead compares it to the value retrieved from the property getter function as follows:

```C#
public class Person : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;

    string givenNames;
    public string GivenNames
    {
        get => givenNames;
        set
        {
            // Comparison uses the property getter
            if (value != GivenNames)
            {
                givenNames = value;
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.GivenNames);
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.FullName);
            }
        }
    }

    string familyName;
    public string FamilyName
    {
        get => familyName;
        set 
        {
            // Comparison uses the property getter
            if (value != FamilyName)
            {
                familyName = value;
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.FamilyName);
                OnPropertyChanged(InternalEventArgsCache.FullName);
            }
        }
    }

    public string FullName => $"{GivenNames} {FamilyName}";

    protected void OnPropertyChanged(PropertyChangedEventArgs eventArgs)
    {
        PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, eventArgs);
    }
}

internal static class InternalEventArgsCache
{
    internal static PropertyChangedEventArgs FamilyName = new PropertyChangedEventArgs("FamilyName");
    internal static PropertyChangedEventArgs FullName = new PropertyChangedEventArgs("FullName");
    internal static PropertyChangedEventArgs GivenNames = new PropertyChangedEventArgs("GivenNames");
}
```

When used in conjunction with NHibernate or Entity Framework this causes a number of unintended consequences. E.g. when the property getter is a proxy, it may cause the proxy to be initialized from the database prior to being set. For collection based properties, this can cause a huge performance impact.

If possible, PropertyChanged.Fody should be modified to retrieve the value from the auto-generated backing field as currently documented to avoid the function call overhead and prevent unanticipated behavior when used in conjunction with ORM's like NHibernate and Entity Framework. Using the DoNotNotify attribute may work in some instances, but not others. The DoNotCheckForEquality attribute can be used to skip the equality check, but it may also cause other performance issues.

Given the popularity of these ORM's I would consider this a major improvement for the benefit of all who use this library.

Dispose SDK SelectObjectContentResponse, honor cancellation, and stop buffering the full result in SelectObjectContentAsync

_Merged from two independently-reported findings (finders `I-provider-s3` and `I-concurrency`) describing the same root-cause defect in `AwsS3StorageClient.SelectObjectContentAsync`: the SDK response leak, the unbounded in-memory buffering, and the ignored `CancellationToken`._

## Summary

`AwsS3StorageClient.SelectObjectContentAsync` opens an AWS SDK S3 Select event stream (`Amazon.S3.Model.SelectObjectContentResponse`, an `IDisposable` that owns the underlying HTTP response and the live `Payload` event-stream socket), copies every `RecordsEvent` payload into an unbounded `MemoryStream`, and returns only that buffer. The SDK response is **never disposed** — not by this method, not by the `S3SelectObjectContentResult` it returns, and not by any downstream caller. Because the returned stream is a detached `MemoryStream`, disposing it downstream does **not** release the SDK response, so the underlying HTTP response / streaming socket (owned by `response.Payload`, an `ISelectObjectContentEventStream : IDisposable`) leaks on every call until GC finalization. Two secondary problems compound this: the completion wait ignores the `CancellationToken`, and the entire filtered result set is materialized in RAM, defeating S3 Select's streaming design.

## Location

- `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.Provider.S3/Internal/AwsS3StorageClient.cs:2518` — SDK `response` captured, never disposed
- `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.Provider.S3/Internal/AwsS3StorageClient.cs:2519`-`2536` / `:2526` — unbounded `MemoryStream` buffering; only `outputStream` returned
- `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.Provider.S3/Internal/AwsS3StorageClient.cs:2531` — `await tcs.Task` ignores the `CancellationToken`
- `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.Provider.S3/Internal/AwsS3StorageClient.cs:2534` — returns result carrying no reference to the SDK response
- `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.Provider.S3/Internal/S3SelectObjectContentResult.cs` (`:3`) — result record wraps only the buffered stream (`EventStream` + `ContentType`), no ownership of the SDK response
- `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.Provider.S3/S3StorageService.cs:918`-`923` — copies the buffered stream into the abstraction `SelectObjectContentResponse`
- Consumer: `src/IntegratedS3/IntegratedS3.AspNetCore/Endpoints/IntegratedS3EndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:10975` (`await using var eventStream = _response.EventStream;` disposes only the `MemoryStream`)

## Details

```csharp
var response = await _s3.SelectObjectContentAsync(sdkRequest, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
var outputStream = new MemoryStream();
if (response.Payload is { } payload)
{
    var tcs = new TaskCompletionSource(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
    payload.RecordsEventReceived += (_, args) =>
    {
        if (args.EventStreamEvent?.Payload is { } recordPayload)
            recordPayload.CopyTo(outputStream);   // whole result buffered in RAM
    };
    payload.EndEventReceived += (_, _) => tcs.TrySetResult();
    payload.ExceptionReceived += (_, args) => tcs.TrySetException(args.EventStreamException);
    payload.StartProcessing();
    await tcs.Task.ConfigureAwait(false);   // no cancellationToken observed
    outputStream.Position = 0;
}
return new S3SelectObjectContentResult(EventStream: outputStream, ContentType: "application/octet-stream");
// `response` (IDisposable, owns the network event stream) goes out of scope here — never disposed on any path.
```

`response` is a local; after the method returns it is unreferenced and only reclaimed by the finalizer. `S3SelectObjectContentResult` carries just the `MemoryStream`; `S3StorageService.SelectObjectContentAsync` (S3StorageService.cs:918-923) copies that stream into the abstraction `SelectObjectContentResponse`; and the endpoint (`IntegratedS3EndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:10975`) does `await using var eventStream = _response.EventStream;` — which disposes only the `MemoryStream`. Nothing disposes the SDK response or its live `Payload` stream.

This is provably inconsistent with the codebase's own established pattern. `GetObjectAsync` (`AwsS3StorageClient.cs:747`) threads the SDK `response` into `S3GetObjectResult` as the `responseWrapper`, and `S3GetObjectResult` wraps the content in a purpose-built `ResponseOwnedStream` (`S3GetObjectResult.cs:14-18,65-100`) whose `DisposeAsync`/`Dispose` explicitly disposes the owning SDK response after the inner stream. The Select path omits this wrapper entirely — `S3SelectObjectContentResult` is a bare record holding only the `MemoryStream`.

**Failure scenario:** a client issues `POST /{bucket}/{key}?select&select-type=2` against an S3-backed provider. The endpoint (`SelectObjectContentStreamResult.ExecuteAsync`) streams and disposes `response.EventStream` — but that is the detached `MemoryStream`, not the SDK `SelectObjectContentResponse`. The SDK response and its `Payload`/`ISelectObjectContentEventStream` (which own the HTTP response and the streaming socket) are never released deterministically. Every Select request therefore leaks one HTTP connection. Under sustained or concurrent Select traffic the SDK's connection pool is exhausted (connection starvation / socket leak) and subsequent S3 operations stall or fail.

Additional problems on this path:
- **Unbounded buffering:** the full result is copied into a `MemoryStream` before returning, so a large filtered result can OOM the process (heap/LOH pressure, GC-pressure DoS vector) and defeats streaming.
- **Cancellation not wired:** `cancellationToken` is passed to the initial `SelectObjectContentAsync` call but not to `StartProcessing()` or the `TaskCompletionSource`; `await tcs.Task` (line 2531) does not observe the token. If the upstream event stream stalls without firing `EndEventReceived`/`ExceptionReceived`, the wait blocks forever, pinning the leaked connection and the request. `StartProcessing()` runs on a background thread and the token is never wired into the TCS, so client cancellation / request abort cannot interrupt it.
- **Exception path also leaks:** if `ExceptionReceived` faults the TCS (or any exception propagates), or when `Payload is null`, `response` is still never disposed.

## Impact

Per-request HTTP connection/socket leak on a reachable Select endpoint path. Every SelectObjectContent request against an S3-backed provider leaks the SDK response object and its underlying HTTP connection until GC finalization; repeated or concurrent Select calls exhaust the SDK connection pool, eventually stalling all outbound S3 traffic from the process. Compounded by unbounded in-memory buffering (OOM / GC-pressure DoS risk on large result sets) and unresponsive cancellation (a stalled upstream event stream hangs the request indefinitely).

## Suggested fix

Mirror the `S3GetObjectResult` / `ResponseOwnedStream` ownership pattern so the SDK `SelectObjectContentResponse` is disposed when the result is disposed. Prefer exposing the SDK event stream directly (wrapped so disposing the result disposes `response`) instead of buffering into a `MemoryStream`. At minimum:

- Give `S3SelectObjectContentResult` ownership of `response` and dispose it in its `Dispose`/`DisposeAsync`, carrying the SDK response through and wrapping the returned stream in the existing `ResponseOwnedStream` so disposal cascades; have the abstraction `SelectObjectContentResponse.DisposeAsync` chain to it. Alternatively `using`/`await using` the SDK `response` after the drain completes.
- Wrap the body in `try`/`catch` so `response` is disposed on every path, including exceptions and the `Payload is null` case.
- Honor the `CancellationToken` on the completion wait, e.g. `await tcs.Task.WaitAsync(cancellationToken)` or register `cancellationToken.Register(() => tcs.TrySetCanceled(cancellationToken))`, and abort/stop payload processing on cancellation.
