RFC: Deepen the Spark Canvas core with a typed bridge + session director

> ✅ Design phase complete. Hardened via `/design-interface` after a 4-way parallel exploration. The interface below is the chosen design; rejected alternatives are documented at the bottom for posterity.

## Problem

The Spark Canvas feature has two distinct sources of architectural friction that are intertwined enough to warrant a single refactor:

### Friction 1 — Stringly-typed C#↔JS protocol smeared across three layers

The ~14 protocol action verbs (8 outbound, 6 inbound) are handled in three different places:

- `SparkCanvasView.xaml.cs` peeks at raw JSON strings (`message.Contains("\"action\":\"ready\"")`) — protocol knowledge leaking into the View layer (lines 135–176)
- `SparkCanvasViewModel.OnCanvasMessage` parses `JsonDocument` and switches on the `action` field (lines 201–240)
- `web/spark/events.js` `handleHostMessage` handles the JS side independently

Both the WPF (`TerminalHost`) and Avalonia (`TerminalHost.Avalonia`) projects carry duplicate copies of this plumbing (705 + 703 lines of VM, near-identical). No protocol versioning, no schema, no documentation file. Adding a new verb requires coordinated edits across three layers with no compile-time check that all sides agree.

### Friction 2 — Implicit session/mode state machine scattered across six methods

A real state machine — `Empty → Single(sessionId) → Multi | Replay(filePath)` and back — is encoded as two implicit booleans (`_isMultiMode`, `CurrentSessionId == null`) inside `SparkCanvasViewModel`. Transitions and event-routing decisions are spread across `OpenSession`, `OnCanvasReady`, `AutoConnectToActiveSession`, `LoadMultiMode`, `LoadJsonlFileAsync`, `OnActivityEvent`.

Every activity-event handler re-derives "should I forward this event?" from those two booleans:

```csharp
if (!_isMultiMode && CurrentSessionId == null && evt.Type == ActivityEventType.SessionStart)
    OpenSession(evt.SessionId);  // auto-connect as a side effect inside the filter
if (!_isMultiMode && (CurrentSessionId == null || evt.SessionId != CurrentSessionId)) return;
```

### Why this makes the codebase harder to navigate and maintain

- **Shallow modules**: `SparkCanvasViewModel` is 705 lines but its public surface is dominated by transport and serialization plumbing rather than UI state. The Avalonia VM duplicates 99% of this (~703 lines), so every protocol or state-machine change must be made twice.
- **Integration risk in the seams**: protocol knowledge (verb strings, JSON shape, threading) spans View → VM → JS with no single source of truth. The View even string-peeks JSON envelopes to short-circuit the ready handshake.
- **Untestable today**: zero unit tests cover `SparkCanvasViewModel`. State-machine bugs hide in interactions (auto-connect firing twice, events leaking across sessions in multi-mode, replay clobbering live state) only exercisable by running the WPF/Avalonia app with a live Claude Code session.
- **Three near-duplicate serializers** (`SerializeState`, `SerializeStateForReplay`, plus an inline placeholder DTO inside `LoadMultiMode`) diverge subtly and the divergence is undocumented.

## Proposed Interface

Hybrid centered on a strict ports-and-adapters orchestrator with typed wire-level protocol records and explicit named host commands. The state machine is a sealed `CanvasState` discriminated union; transitions are pure functions of `(state, trigger)`.

### Three new ports (in `TerminalHost.Core/Interfaces/Spark/`)

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Bidirectional canvas channel. Production wraps WebView2 / Avalonia.WebView.
/// Test impl records outbound messages and exposes Inject(inbound) for inputs.
/// Adapter owns UI-thread marshaling and the ready-handshake / pre-ready queue —
/// orchestrator assumes all callbacks arrive on a single logical thread.
/// </summary>
public interface ICanvasTransport
{
    Task SendAsync(CanvasOutbound message);             // safe pre-ready (queues)
    event EventHandler<CanvasInbound>? Received;        // typed; raised on UI thread
    event EventHandler? Ready;                          // raised once
    bool IsReady { get; }
}

/// <summary>
/// Unified session-discovery + replay-loading surface. Merges what TimelineService
/// + SessionActivityService + TranscriptParserService expose to the canvas into one
/// canvas-shaped query API. The "three diverging serializers" problem dies here:
/// the orchestrator asks the catalog for a SessionSnapshot and serializes once.
/// </summary>
public interface ISessionCatalog
{
    IReadOnlyList<SessionListItem> List();              // merges live + activity-tracked
    SessionSnapshot? GetSnapshot(string sessionId);     // null if unknown
    Task<SessionSnapshot?> LoadReplayAsync(string jsonlPath, CancellationToken ct);
    Task EnrichAsync(string sessionId, CancellationToken ct); // pull missing model from transcript
}

/// <summary>
/// Narrow slice of IConfigurationService. The orchestrator may NOT mutate
/// any other setting via this port.
/// </summary>
public interface IThemeStore
{
    string Load();                                      // default "holographic"
    void Save(string theme);
}
```

`ISessionActivityService` (the off-thread `ActivityEventProcessed` source) is consumed directly — it is already a Core port with fakes. The orchestrator subscribes via the transport adapter, which marshals events to the UI thread before they reach orchestrator code.

### Closed-record wire vocabularies (in `TerminalHost.Core/Spark/`)

Closed unions earn their keep across the C#↔JS serialization boundary because the C# compiler enforces that every new verb is handled at every switch site. Host-initiated commands (next section) use explicit named methods instead — they are stable, grep-friendly, and don't cross a serialization boundary.

```csharp
public abstract record CanvasOutbound
{
    public sealed record Clear                                                 : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record LoadState(SessionPayload Session)                     : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record LoadReplay(SessionPayload Session, IReadOnlyList<EventPayload> Events) : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record Event(EventPayload Payload)                           : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record SetTheme(string Theme)                                : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record SetSession(string? Id, string? DisplayName)           : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record SessionList(IReadOnlyList<SessionListItem> Sessions)  : CanvasOutbound;
    public sealed record LoadMultiState(IReadOnlyList<SessionPayload> Sessions): CanvasOutbound;
}

public abstract record CanvasInbound
{
    public sealed record Ready                                                 : CanvasInbound;
    public sealed record SelectSession(string SessionId)                       : CanvasInbound;
    public sealed record RefreshSessions                                       : CanvasInbound;
    public sealed record RequestMultiMode                                      : CanvasInbound;
    public sealed record ExitMultiMode                                         : CanvasInbound;
    public sealed record ThemeChanged(string Theme)                            : CanvasInbound;
}
```

